<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|generic>

<\body>
  Practicamente al final de la introduccion, se proponen los objetivos de
  este paper:

  <\enumerate-numeric>
    <item>determine the clustering properties of galaxy clusters from
    state-of-the-art simulations where galaxy properties are de- rived from
    semi-analytical modelling

    <item>test how much the clustering properties evidenced on ideal mock
    catalogues can be recovered when degrading the redshift information to
    reproduce the photometric uncertainty on redshift expected in future
    cluster experiments. (Our objective is to optimize the analysis of
    cluster clustering from forthcoming cluster catalogues that will be
    issued from the ongoing and future large multiband photometric surveys.)
  </enumerate-numeric>

  <section|Secciones>

  <subsection|Introduccion.>

  \;

  \;

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><em|One of the major challenges in modern cosmology is to explain
    the observed acceleration of the cosmic expansion, determining if it is
    due to a positive cosmological constant, a time\Uvarying dark energy
    component or a modified theory of gravity.>

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>Esta es la excusa para el estudio, es decir le da pie a la
      necesidad de hacer el trabajo, en este caso el estudio seria util
      enmarcado en la necesidad de explicar la expansion acelerada del
      universo.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item>Para responder a estas preguntas ellos lo que plantean es utilizar
    los clusters galacticos:

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item><em|<em|<em|In fact galaxy cluster counts as a function of
      redshift and mass are sensitive to dark energy through their dependence
      on the volume element and on the structure growth rate >.> One
      intrinsic difficulty in constraining the cosmological models with
      galaxy cluster counts comes from uncertainties in cluster mass
      estimates and on the difficulty to calibrate related mass proxies.>
    </itemize-minus>

    <item>Para hacer los analisis anteriores proponen utilizar el estudio de
    la funcion de correlacion de los clusters.\ 

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item><em|Cluster clustering can be measured through the twopoint
      correlation function, the Fourier transform of the power spectrum,
      which is one of the most successful statistics for analysing clustering
      processes.>

      <item><em|In cosmology, it is a standard tool to test models of
      structure formation and evolution. The cluster correlation function is
      much higher than that of galaxies. This is a consequence of the fact
      that more massive haloes correspond to higher and rarer density
      fluctuations, which have a higher cor- relation amplitude.>

      <item><em|Galaxy clusters are associated to the most massive virialised
      dark matter haloes, and as a conse quence their correlation function is
      strongly amplified.>
    </itemize-minus>

    <item>Ellos quieren utilizar los datos de Sloan, sin embargo hay
    problemas con la estima de redshifts fotometricos.

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>However, among the several difficulties to be overcome in us- ing
      clusters as cosmological probes is the impact of photometric redshift
      errors.\ 

      <item>While some of these surveys have (in general partially) a
      spectroscopic follow up, many forthcoming large galaxy surveys will
      have only the photometric information in multiple bands, so that their
      cluster catalogues will be built on the basis of state-of-the-art
      photometric redshifts.\ 

      <item>Using those instead of real redshifts will cause a
      <strong|positional uncertainty> along the line of sight inducing a
      damping of clustering at small scales and a smearing of the acoustic
      peak
    </itemize-minus>
  </itemize-dot>

  <subsection|Simulaciones>

  Como se vio anteriormente, ellos quieren utilizar funciones de correlacion
  para medir el clustering de los clusters. Asi que por ende, deben utilizar
  alguna simulacion.

  We use a public light-cone catalogue constructed using a semi-analytic
  model of galaxy formation onto the N-body dark matter halo merger trees of
  the Millennium Simulation, based on a lambda cold dark matter
  (\<Lambda\>CDM) cosmological model with the following parameters:

  <\equation*>
    \<Omega\><rsub|M>,\<Omega\><rsub|\<Lambda\>>,\<Omega\><rsub|b>,h=0.25,0.75,0.045,0.73
  </equation*>

  corresponding to the first year results from the Wilkinson Microwave
  Anisotropy Probe.

  The area covered by the light-cone is <math|500 deg<rsup|2>> ; the final
  mock catalogue is magnitude\Ulimited to <math|H = 24> (to mimic the Euclid
  completeness) with a maximum redshift at <math|z = 3>.

  <subsection|Funciones de Correlacion>

  Aqui se hace un breve repaso de las funciones de correlacion.

  \;

  <\equation*>
    d P<rsub|1 2>=n<rsup|2><around*|[|1+\<xi\><around*|(|r|)>|]>d V<rsub|1> d
    V<rsub|2>
  </equation*>

  Aqui <math|d P<rsub|12>> es la probabilidad de encontrar dos objetos en los
  volumenes <math|d V<rsub|1>,d V<rsub|2>> separados a una distancia
  <math|r>. <math|n> es la densidad media mientras que <math|\<xi\>> es la
  funcion de correlacion que mide el exceso de probabilidad de encontrar un
  par en relacion a una distribucion de Poisson.

  El estimador que se utiliza para calcular la funcion de correlacion es el
  de Landy & Szalay.

  <\equation*>
    \<xi\><around*|(|r|)>=<frac|D D<around*|(|r|)>-2D R<around*|(|r|)>+R
    R<around*|(|r|)>|R R<around*|(|r|)>>
  </equation*>

  Se dan unos cuantos argumentos para utilizar este estimador:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>less sensitive to the size of the random catalogue and better in
    handling edge corrections

    <item>Se argumenta ademas el paper de Kerscher 2000 que compara la
    performance de distintos estimadores para la funcion de correlacion.

    <item><math|D D>: is the number of data-data pairs counted within a
    spherical shell of radius <math|r> and <math|r + d r>

    <item><math|D R>: refers to the number of data-random pairs

    <item>and RR refers to the random-random pairs
  </itemize-minus>

  Ahora se indica algo muy importante, que deacuerdo a Peebles, la funcion de
  correlacion en el espacio real, sigue una Power Law:

  <\equation*>
    \<xi\><around*|(|r|)>=<around*|(|<frac|r|r<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|-\<gamma\>>
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><math|r0> : es la longitud de correlacion y <math|\<gamma\>> es la
    pendiente.

    <item>The random catalogues we use reproduce the cluster redshift
    <strong|selection function>, estimated by smoothing the cluster redshift
    distribution through a kernel density estimation method.
  </itemize-minus>

  <\big-figure|<image|<tuple|<#>|png>|1par|||>>
    Distribucion de los redshifts comparadas con su catalogo simulado y los
    datos.
  </big-figure>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item>Sobre los errores: Errors are calculated from the covariance
    matrices using the jackknife resampling method
  </itemize-dot>

  <subsection|Estima de la funcion de correlacion con redshifts fotometricos>

  <subsection|Recobrando la funcion de correlacion en el espacio real.>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item>In the following we will take into account separately the line of
    sight <math|\<pi\>> and the transverse <math|r<rsub|p>> components of the
    two-point correlation function.

    <item>Photometric redshifts affect only the line of sight component,
    introducing an anisotropy in the <math|\<pi\> \<minus\> r<rsub|p>> plane:
    the redshift-space correlation function will have a lower amplitude and
    steeper slope with respect to the real-space correlation function.

    <item>Lo siguiente es del paper de Arnalte (Recovering the real-space
    correlation function from photometric redshift surveys)[Creo que explica
    mejor]: The large errors in redshift and hence in the line-of-sight
    positions produce two effects that have to be considered.

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>On one side, these random shifts in position erase correlations
      between points, and hence <math|\<xi\> (r)> measured according to (1)
      (Landy Szalay) would be much lower than the real <math|\<xi\> (r)>.

      <item>On the other side as the shifts are only in the line-of-sight
      direction, isotropy of the distribution is lost. Correlation is only
      lost along the longitudinal direction, but it is conserved in the
      transverse plane.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item>En lineas generales lo que va a hacer ahora es definir las
    direcciones <math|\<sigma\>,\<pi\>>. Aqui <math|\<pi\>> esta a lo largo
    de la linea de la visual. Tipicamente se define como:

    <math|<with|font-series|bold|s=x<rsub|2>-x<rsub|1>> > ;
    <math|<with|font-series|bold|l=<frac|1|2><around*|(|x<rsub|1>+x<rsub|2>|)>>>

    <item>Tras definir estos dos vectores, uno para la separacion espacial
    entre los objetos: <math|<with|font-series|bold|s>> mientras que el otro
    es para denotar la LOS: <math|<with|font-series|bold|l>>.

    <item>Entonces se definen:

    <math|\<pi\>=<frac|<with|font-series|bold|s\<cdummy\>l>|l>> : Componente
    paralela a LOS\ 

    <math|\<sigma\>> tiene que ser un escalar del modulo de la componente
    perpendicular.
  </itemize-dot>

  Tal y como dijo Diego, es trivial ir desde la funcion de correlacion 3D a
  la 2D:

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|p><around*|(|\<sigma\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|-\<infty\>><rsup|\<infty\>>\<xi\><around*|(|\<sigma\>,\<pi\>|)>d
    \<pi\>
  </equation*>

  Esta expresion se reformula como:

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|p><around*|(|r<rsub|p>|)>=2<big|int><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|\<infty\>>\<xi\><around*|(|r|)><around*|(|r<rsup|2>-\<sigma\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1/2>
  </equation*>

  A partir de aca la idea es invertir para encontrar la funcion de
  correlacion. Debe elegirse un limite finito para trabajar en la realidad.

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|p><around*|(|\<sigma\>,\<pi\><rsub|max>|)>=2<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<pi\><rsub|max>>\<xi\><around*|(|\<sigma\>,\<pi\>|)>d
    \<pi\>
  </equation*>

  El resultado de la inversion nos daria:

  <\equation*>
    \<xi\><around*|(|r|)>=<frac|-1|\<pi\>><big|int><rsub|r><rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|max>>w<rprime|'><around*|(|\<sigma\>|)><around*|(|\<sigma\><rsup|2>-r<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1/2>d
    \<sigma\>
  </equation*>

  Observe que hay ahora definidos <math|\<sigma\><rsub|max>> y
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|max>>.
</body>

